<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walhalla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Walhalla memorial</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walhalla is a hall of fame in the form of a Greek Doric temple, built for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-i-of-bavaria">Ludwig I of Bavaria</a> on a hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> near Regensburg. Designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-von-klenze">Leo von Klenze</a> and built between 1830 and 1842, it holds busts and plaques honouring prominent figures of German-speaking history and culture. It expressed the romantic nationalism of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> not yet politically united.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walhalla.jpg" alt="Walhalla" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wolfgang Pehlemann · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walhalla_wie_Parthenon_zu_Ehren_bedeutender_Personen_-_erbaut_1842_-_K%C3%B6nig_Ludwig_I_-_Foto_Wolfgang_Pehlemann_DSCN2430.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
